1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,720
Suddenly a sound rings out, stopping you in your tracks.
一声铃响突然响起，让你停下手里的事。

2
00:00:04,720 --> 00:00:08,410
Panicking, you search for where it could be coming from.
惊慌中，你寻找声音的来源。

3
00:00:08,410 --> 00:00:18,110
It's your phone, and if you're like a quarter of 18 to 34-year-olds in a recent British study, you probably won't answer it.
是你的手机，如果你像最近一项英国研究中四分之一的18至34岁人群一样，你可能不会接听。

4
00:00:18,110 --> 00:00:24,860
The same study found that 70% of people in this age group prefer text messages to phone calls.
同一研究发现，这个年龄段70%的人更喜欢发短信而不是打电话。

5
00:00:24,860 --> 00:00:28,280
Why do so many people hate phone calls?
为什么这么多人讨厌打电话？

6
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:41,170
While previous generations grew up using landlines to talk to their friends, smartphone-equipped younger people have grown up accustomed to using text messages, group chats and voice notes for social conversations.
前几代人用固定电话与朋友聊天，而拥有智能手机的年轻人则习惯了用短信、群聊和语音留言进行社交对话。

7
00:00:41,170 --> 00:00:47,550
What these media have in common is that they are asynchronous – they don't need to happen in real time.
这些媒体的共同点是它们是异步的——它们不需要实时发生。

8
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:54,830
When you can craft a reply free of interruption, you have greater control over your contributions to a conversation.
当你可以在不受干扰的情况下精心回复时，你就能更好地控制自己在对话中的发言。

9
00:00:54,830 --> 00:00:58,300
It also means that you can reply when it's best for you.
这也意味着你可以在最适合自己的时候回复。

10
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:08,320
Synchronous communication, like phone calls or face-to-face conversations, can lead people to feel a loss of control and the corresponding anxiety.
同步通信，比如电话或面对面交谈，会让人感到失控并产生相应的焦虑。

11
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:16,960
Coupled to this is that fact that many young people report associating phone calls, particularly those without prior warning, with bad news.
与此相关的是，许多年轻人报告说，他们将电话（尤其是没有事先警告的电话）与坏消息联系在一起。

12
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:21,400
This means that new social codes are being established.
这意味着新的社交规范正在建立。

13
00:01:21,400 --> 00:01:26,190
Many people will now text someone to see if they're available to take a phone call.
现在许多人会先发短信询问对方是否方便接电话。

14
00:01:26,190 --> 00:01:32,970
If someone doesn't feel able to sum something up in a few short messages, they might leave a long voice note.
如果有人觉得无法用几条短信把事情说清楚，他们可能会留一条很长的语音留言。

15
00:01:32,970 --> 00:01:42,610
Could it be that not wanting to intrude on someone with a sudden phone call is just a question of good manners and respecting someone's boundaries?
不想用突如其来的电话打扰别人，是否只是礼貌和尊重他人界限的问题？

16
00:01:42,610 --> 00:01:48,650
Maybe, but this doesn't mean that anxiety around communication has been eliminated.
也许吧，但这并不意味着围绕沟通的焦虑已经消除。

17
00:01:48,650 --> 00:02:00,320
Texting may be asynchronous, but many people report tensions from being 'left on read' – when you know someone has read your message, but they don't, or won't, reply.
短信可能是异步的，但许多人报告说，因"已读不回"而感到紧张——当你明知某人已读你的消息，但他们没有或不愿回复。

18
00:02:00,320 --> 00:02:11,470
Many would agree with British writer Daisy Buchanan, who reminds us how picking up the phone to talk to someone can be far more effective at relieving tension than a series of awkward messages.
许多人会同意英国作家黛西·布坎南的观点，她提醒我们，拿起电话与人交谈在缓解紧张方面远比一连串尴尬的短信有效。

19
00:02:11,470 --> 00:02:20,820
So, are these new social codes better at respecting boundaries, or are they just creating a whole new set of potential anxieties?
那么，这些新的社交规范是更好地尊重了界限，还是只是制造了一整套新的潜在焦虑？
